The goal of our project is to create an interactive player-versus-computer Texas Hold'em Poker game using Python. As students interested in programming and game development, the motivation behind our project is to explore how algorithms, probability, and decision-making can be implemented in a realistic and engaging card game. Poker is a game that combines chance, strategy, and critical thinking, making it an excellent opportunity to apply programming concepts while creating an entertaining experience for users. Through this project, we aim to develop a game that not only allows players to enjoy a classic card game but also demonstrates how computers can make intelligent decisions based on available information.

The game begins by creating and shuffling a standard 52-card deck, then dealing two private cards to both the player and the computer, along with five community cards that are revealed throughout the game. Each player starts with a fixed amount of virtual chips, and blinds are used to create an initial pot before cards are dealt. During each round, the user can choose to fold, call, or raise, while the computer evaluates its hand strength and estimates its probability of winning before making its own decision. To make gameplay more realistic, the computer can occasionally bluff by making aggressive moves even when holding weaker cards. At the end of each hand, the game evaluates the best possible five-card poker hand that each participant can make using their private cards and the community cards, determines the winner, and awards the pot accordingly.

The project involves creating functions to generate and manage the deck, deal cards, evaluate poker hands, handle betting rounds, track chip counts, and control the computer's decision-making process. Additional features include a hand-ranking system that recognizes combinations such as pairs, straights, flushes, and full houses, as well as a probability-based artificial intelligence system that allows the computer to make strategic decisions. Overall, this project combines object-oriented programming, probability, and game logic to create an engaging poker experience that showcases the practical applications of Python programming.
